{"cursor": 127, "kind": "session_update", "sessionId": "ses_eef15e1feffe00E5rzvPe6xrJl", "ts": 1791285466.504, "update": {"content": [{"content": {"text": "Read file /Users/ylmacstudio/.config/opencode/skills/opencode-kaola-project-runner/SKILL.md, lines 1-166\n1: ---\n2: name: opencode-kaola-project-runner\n3: description: Use when the controlling Agent should communicate with an OpenCode main conversation through an exact owned ACP session by starting it, reading evidence, sending Agent-selected prompts or keys, reading replies, and stopping only that session.\n4: ---\n5: \n6: # OpenCode Kaola Project Runner\n7: \n8: This Skill is a communication driver for OpenCode. It gives the controlling Agent a\n9: measured ACP channel to one exact owned session; it does not choose commands, Workflow modes, cadence, state, approvals,\n10: retries, or completion policy. The separate Skill `kaola-project-runner` (display name Project\n11: Runner) is the main orchestrator when a host Agent is supervising workers; this Skill stays\n12: transport-only.\n13: \n14: ## Transport\n15: \n16: ACP is the only transport (Issue #130). The ACP command is `opencode acp`; prompts travel over its stdio JSON-RPC, never shell eval. Login is a human act outside the Runner and needs no terminal. This platform's ACP quirks are in [references/acp.md](references/acp.md) — open it when a quirk matters. A request for the retired PTY transport is refused with `transport-pty-retired` and changes nothing.\n17: \n18: | `mutation_status` | Safe interpretation |\n19: |---|---|\n20: | `not_started` | No prompt write began. |\n21: | `accepted` | The agent accepted the prompt. |\n22: | `in_progress` | Work may already be mutating state. |\n23: | `completed` | The turn reached a reported stop reason. |\n24: | `unknown` | Partial mutation cannot be ruled out. |\n25: \n26: Runner never auto-falls back or resends, and never restarts or resumes a session on its own. Read the receipt and decide whether another prompt is appropriate.\n27: \n28: ## Communication loop\n29: \n30: Resolve this Skill's installed directory once and call its scripts by absolute path — the install\n31: destination may contain spaces, and the user's project is passed only through `--repo` (relative\n32: paths in these references resolve against the Skill, never the project cwd). Progressive\n33: disclosure: open a reference only when the current operation needs it, run the scripts and never read their source, and prefer bounded\n34: receipts (`observe`, `status`, `capture --lines`) over whole-history dumps — `capture --full` is an\n35: explicit, unbounded request:\n36: \n37: ```bash\n38: SKILL_DIR=\"/absolute/path/to/opencode-kaola-project-runner\"   # the directory containing this file\n39: REPO=\"/abs/canonical/root\"   # canonical Git root, not a linked worktree\n40: SESSION=\"<exact-name>\"   # under Project Runner: <platform>-<CODE>-i<ISSUE>-<purpose>\n41: \"$SKILL_DIR/scripts/runtime-tmux.sh\" preflight --repo \"$REPO\" --session \"$SESSION\"\n42: \"$SKILL_DIR/scripts/runtime-tmux.sh\" start --repo \"$REPO\" --session \"$SESSION\"\n43: \"$SKILL_DIR/scripts/runtime-tmux.sh\" observe --repo \"$REPO\" --session \"$SESSION\"\n44: \"$SKILL_DIR/scripts/runtime-tmux.sh\" capture --repo \"$REPO\" --session \"$SESSION\" --lines 200\n45: ```\n46: \n47: The controlling Agent owns model selection for each `start`. This Skill declares its per-run\n48: presets — `--tier default` (**DeepSeek V4.1 Flash**: `opencode-go/deepseek-v4.1-flash`, no Runner effort override) only — and `default` applies whenever nothing more specific was chosen. Pass\n49: another `--tier` when the human or an existing authorization — the default-authorized Worker pool included —\n50: names that preset; never infer one from code size, failures, elapsed time, or your own assessment.\n51: An undeclared name is refused (`tier-not-declared`), never resolved to `default`.\n52: \n53: An explicit user model choice always wins: pass it with `--model ID`, adding `--effort LEVEL` only\n54: when the user also named an effort. A bare explicit `--model` leaves the runtime's native effort\n55: alone — never attach a preset's effort to a different model. If the user picks a model ID that\n56: already encodes effort or Fast (such as a `-fast` variant), pass it as-is; the Runner does not\n57: invent extra effort or Fast configuration for it.\n58: \n59: Fast is OFF by default. Pass `--fast on` only on an explicit user request for Fast; this platform's\n60: Fast support: no native Fast toggle; speed-named catalog models such as zhipuai-coding-plan/glm-5.3-flash are explicit model choices, not a Fast switch. Fast and tier are independent selections. When a native fast model\n61: ID is what the user explicitly selected, it counts as the explicit Fast selection — report the\n62: conflict honestly if it is also passed with `--fast off`.\n63: \n64: `preflight` and `start` report the requested and resolved selection, and `start` what it applied\n65: (`config_application`, `effective_selection`), including unavailable, unsupported, and unknown\n66: outcomes; `observe`/`status` show the agent's `configOptions` `currentValue`. `--resume`/`--continue`\n67: preserve the saved native session model and effort unless the caller supplies `--tier`, `--model`,\n68: or `--effort`; Fast stays a per-run request (off unless explicitly on). All of this is per-run\n69: input and never rewrites global CLI configuration. A mismatch or unreadable actual model remains\n70: evidence for the Agent and does not disable the communication channel.\n71: \n72: `preflight` reports runtime and optional Kaola carrier evidence. Missing Workflow commands,\n73: configuration health, account state, activity hints, or a changed\n74: snapshot do not authorize or block starting the CLI communication channel.\n75: \n76: Use the evidence internally to choose the next communication action. Do not narrate raw holder,\n77: process, snapshot, model, or activity fields in user progress updates; report only visible\n78: task progress, an actual transport failure, or a decision that genuinely needs the user.\n79: \n80: After reading current evidence, the controlling Agent chooses what to send:\n81: \n82: ```bash\n83: \"$SKILL_DIR/scripts/runtime-tmux.sh\" send --repo \"$REPO\" --session \"$SESSION\" --text '<agent-selected prompt>'\n84: \"$SKILL_DIR/scripts/runtime-tmux.sh\" observe --repo \"$REPO\" --session \"$SESSION\"\n85: \"$SKILL_DIR/scripts/runtime-tmux.sh\" capture --repo \"$REPO\" --session \"$SESSION\" --lines 200\n86: ```\n87: \n88: ## Steering a running turn\n89: \n90: No native mid-turn entry has been verified on OpenCode's ACP surface, so a bare\n91: `steer` refuses and writes nothing. That is an unverified capability, not a\n92: proven absence: nothing here says the entry does not exist. The available path\n93: is the composite, which you choose explicitly:\n94: \n95: ```bash\n96: \"$SKILL_DIR/scripts/runtime-tmux.sh\" steer --repo \"$REPO\" --session \"$SESSION\" \\\n97:   --steer-mode interrupt --text '<redirection>'\n98: ```\n99: \n100: It **cancels** the running turn, confirms it stopped, then sends your text as the\n101: next turn on the same session, which keeps the conversation's context. That is\n102: interrupted-then-continued, never injection: work in progress stops and may have\n103: left partial side effects (`side_effects_possible`). An unconfirmed cancel sends\n104: nothing and reports `unknown`. See [references/steering.md](references/steering.md).\n105: \n106: ## Cancel and permissions\n107: \n108: `key --key escape` (or `cancel`) cancels the running turn; there are no other native keys, menus,\n109: or editor replacement. Settle a pending permission `request_id` with\n110: `permit [--request-id ID] --option OPTION_ID` (an option the request offers); omitting `--option`\n111: answers `cancelled`, which denies. Read the resulting output before choosing another action.\n112: \n113: Permission default: Native V2 build full-access default only for fresh managed launches without existing config sources; existing global/project/native config is left intact (including non-permission config). Explicit --permission-mode selects the native build/plan agent via mode and suppresses the default. No auto-permit; remaining requests require an Agent decision..\n114: \n115: When the Agent decides the exact session is finished, end only that owned session:\n116: \n117: ```bash\n118: \"$SKILL_DIR/scripts/runtime-tmux.sh\" stop --repo \"$REPO\" --session \"$SESSION\"\n119: \"$SKILL_DIR/scripts/runtime-tmux.sh\" status --repo \"$REPO\" --session \"$SESSION\"\n120: ```\n121: \n122: Use `--force` only when the Agent explicitly chooses forced containment for this exact owned\n123: session. Never use broad session/process cleanup, and never reconstruct ownership from process\n124: names or fuzzy session matches.\n125: \n126: ## Ending, releasing, and resuming\n127: \n128: The controlling Agent owns every completion judgment; the Runner executes the chosen operation\n129: and reports the true result. These are operation facts, never gates:\n130: \n131: - A finished reply is not a finished task: an `end_turn` event, an idle terminal, or a\n132:   successful `send` receipt never establishes completion.\n133: - `stop` releases the owned runtime (the ACP holder and its agent). It never deletes CLI\n134:   history, session records, work artifacts, or unrelated resources. Judge success by the\n135:   `stop`/`status` result evidence, not by a completed call.\n136: - Before stopping, the Agent may keep available resume facts (platform, canonical repo, any\n137:   reported native session ID, outcome, remaining work) from existing receipts and Workflow\n138:   records. The native session ID is the CLI's own conversation identifier, never the Runner's\n139:   `--session` name. Missing identifiers never block a chosen `stop`.\n140: - Later work resumes by the Agent's choice: `start --resume <native-session-id>`,\n141:   `start --continue` for the platform's latest conversation, or a fresh `start` plus existing\n142:   records where the platform cannot resume.\n143: \n144: ## Optional Kaola Workflow\n145: \n146: Ordinary Workflow-backed work binds `--repo` to the consuming project's canonical Git root and asks this CLI to invoke its installed\n147: `workflow-next`, so that runtime's Workflow creates or recovers the child worktree; this Skill only\n148: transports the exact session. Linked-worktree starts, outer-prepared bundles, and existing-run\n149: recovery are Agent decisions, not transport gates. A session already in a child worktree is advisory: preserve work, then continue,\n150: stop/restart at root, or use another Workflow recovery path. Installation for another runtime alone\n151: does not establish availability here.\n152: \n153: ## Evidence boundary\n154: \n155: - `capture`, events, process facts, permission facts, activity hints, and snapshot changes are\n156:   evidence for the Agent.\n157: - Exact session ownership, platform/repository identity, holder identity, prompt fingerprinting,\n158:   and outbound redaction are transport integrity checks.\n159: - The Runner never classifies evidence into permission to act. The Agent handles every runtime or\n160:   Workflow problem after reading the evidence.\n161: - No invocation implicitly starts `workflow-next`, installs commands, materializes repository files,\n162:   creates a heartbeat, or selects recurring behavior. The Agent may send any of those commands when\n163:   it decides they serve the user's task.\n164: \n165: See [references/platform.md](references/platform.md) for OpenCode launch/observation facts\n166: and [references/acp.md](references/acp.md) for the structured ACP command surface.", "type": "text"}, "type": "content"}], "locations": [{"path": "/Users/ylmacstudio/.config/opencode/skills/opencode-kaola-project-runner/SKILL.md"}], "rawOutput": {"metadata": {"truncated": false}}, "sessionUpdate": "tool_call_update", "status": "completed", "toolCallId": "call_01_dkqm3fd2m3allky15urxyr93"}}
{"cursor": 128, "kind": "session_update", "sessionId": "ses_eef15e1feffe00E5rzvPe6xrJl", "ts": 1791285466.505, "update": {"content": [{"content": {"text": "Read file /Users/ylmacstudio/.config/opencode/skills/kaola-project-runner/SKILL.md, lines 1-264\n1: ---\n2: name: kaola-project-runner\n3: description: \"Use when the controlling Agent should supervise explicitly authorized CLI workers through the ten platform Runner Skills: recover live authorization, dispatch and review work, accept deliveries before finalize, cap live workers at the authorized count, and stop each accepted seat without dropping close-out duties.\"\n4: ---\n5: \n6: # Project Runner\n7: \n8: This Skill is the main control-plane Skill. It is not a platform Runner and has\n9: no transport adapter. The ten platform Runner Skills are workers: they only\n10: identify, start, send, wait, permit, observe, capture, and stop an exact owned\n11: session. Kaola-Workflow, when used, owns worker-side claim, mission ledger,\n12: child worktree, finalize, archive, and sink.\n13: \n14: One Host owns the plan, acceptance and QA.\n15: Helpers do bounded planning, research, QA, synthesis, explicitly scoped light work\n16: or an assignment. They never scale, control workers, run another scheduling loop,\n17: grant permission or accept the project.\n18: You pick presets, write assignments, dispatch, judge originals;\n19: Sideagent nodes reconcile batches: [lifecycle-state.md](references/lifecycle-state.md), [node](references/sideagent-node.md).\n20: Read, search, analyze and do simple authorized work. Larger code, test or doc work goes to workers\n21: unless the human allows self-execution.\n22: \n23: Authorized release/install mechanics may stay Host-owned without an issue or\n24: worker just for them; honor owner allocation and project lifecycle.\n25: \n26: ## Two entry points\n27: \n28: **Standalone/non-Host supervision** - use the loop below without Host event binding.\n29: \n30: **Host** - you are already a named Host session that loaded this Skill through\n31: the native `/kaola-project-runner` Skill invocation (ZCode) or your platform's\n32: measured entry, and supervise workers on event-driven beats. Outer Agents\n33: start or continue the Host through Kaola-Delegator (`kaola-delegator`).\n34: Use existing project plans for role, authorization and lifecycle; this Skill\n35: creates none. Startup and one Host per root (`host-exists`), plus a\n36: Delegator-inquiry sweep (one owned permission event needs no full sweep):\n37: [host-startup.md](references/host-startup.md) ·\n38: [zcode-native-skill-entry.md](references/zcode-native-skill-entry.md) ·\n39: [host-entry-matrix.md](references/host-entry-matrix.md).\n40: A Host (any platform with a `host_skill_entry`) is event-driven: no\n41: Routine, cron, or sleep loop. Its first prompt names its\n42: `platform`/`session`/`repo`; `KAOLA_ACP_DISPATCHER` carries them to its shell.\n43: Every Host assignment uses `--no-wait`, then ends the turn, including direct\n44: Runner/Workflow, continuation, repair and finalize. Beat mechanics - ending the turn as the wait: [references/zcode-host-dispatch.md](references/zcode-host-dispatch.md).\n45: Each inner worker is its own session and process group: an inner stop never\n46: reaches the Host; the Host's stop sweeps only recorded inner sessions.\n47: Grok Bot is not an entry for this Skill: it loads generated `kaola-delegator`,\n48: which starts one Host of any platform that loads this Skill. `--platform grok` is the\n49: Grok CLI worker; `--platform grok-bot` is invalid. Do not create a Grok Bot\n50: Routine to run this Skill. Keep the Host shell cwd at the canonical root: never\n51: `cd` into a worktree finalize/sink may remove; use absolute paths, `git -C`, or\n52: `(cd ... && ...)`.\n53: \n54: ## Consumer-project boundary\n55: \n56: For consumer-project work, the Project Runner checkout, templates, generated\n57: files, and installed Skill payload are read-only. Store project-specific\n58: authorization, heartbeat, and run facts in the consuming project. Do not edit\n59: any of the above unless a human explicitly assigned Project Runner\n60: development.\n61: \n62: ## Authorization\n63: \n64: Recover existing explicit authorization and live work before asking intake\n65: questions. A Skill update or resumed conversation must not restart intake,\n66: workers, claims or assignments already established. Ask only for missing or\n67: conflicting information.\n68: \n69: Record the human's CLI, model/effort, count, and capability restrictions in the\n70: consuming project's run records, not in this Skill. The count is a hard cap on\n71: live worker processes, ACP holders included; a finished seat counts until its\n72: `stop` receipt. Granted open-ended concurrency is its own cap. Do not invent a\n73: quota system: the cap is that count, enforced from `status`/`stop` receipts.\n74: Preset classes ([worker-profiles.md](references/worker-profiles.md)): five\n75: Worker presets are default-authorized outside that cap (permission, not\n76: preference); Elite needs an explicit grant; Expert is complex thinking only,\n77: under an explicit standing grant or fresh user permission per task. Delegate substantial planning, design or\n78: review to an available authorized seat when its profile or the owner's\n79: task-specific judgment makes it a better fit than your own known model/preset.\n80: The heartbeat holds the three Class meanings and a compact capability\n81: summary. Catalog profiles stay in\n82: [profile-catalog.md](references/profile-catalog.md). Exact grants stay in the\n83: heartbeat authorization. At a dispatch decision, project eligible candidates\n84: instead of keeping the profile roster in routine context\n85: ([dispatch-collect.md](references/dispatch-collect.md)).\n86: \n87: Obey owner/project and single-writer rules. Holds stop only source-named actions.\n88: Distinguish implementation prerequisites, merge order and resource occupancy;\n89: a copied Host summary alone establishes no dependency. Unaffected authorized\n90: work proceeds.\n91: \n92: ### Selected platform\n93: \n94: Call the matching platform Runner by its installed directory\n95: `<id>-kaola-project-runner`. Every platform's default transport is ACP.\n96: \n97: ### Progressive disclosure\n98: \n99: This Skill loads on its own. Load one selected worker Skill only at dispatch, a\n100: reference only when the current step needs it, and never read script source or\n101: whole files into context: receipts, hashes, counts and bounded excerpts are the\n102: evidence. Ordinary `observe`, `status` and `capture --lines` receipts stay bounded\n103: (`truncated` names dropped fields); `capture --full` is the only unbounded\n104: request.\n105: Never log in, relogin, switch accounts, or repair credentials. A confirmed limit or account refusal follows [quota-packages.md](references/quota-packages.md); that rule does not change the count cap.\n106: \n107: ### Defaults\n108: \n109: | Item | Default / rule |\n110: |---|---|\n111: | Allowed CLIs | See Authorization above. No authorized task: ask; start no worker or heartbeat. |\n112: | Count | Named CLI without a count: one; it bounds live processes. |\n113: | Model / transport | Platform `--tier default`, Fast off, default transport. Explicit human choices win. Resume preserves saved native choices. |\n114: | Other tiers | Only default is common; tiers are platform-specific/unranked. No automatic model, tier, or transport switch; only owner-authorized model/preset changes stay within runtime. |\n115: | Workflow | On. If explicitly off or unavailable, use authorized PR/verification delivery and disclose the limitation; do not fake Workflow records. |\n116: | Heartbeat | Non-Host: 30 minutes unless specified; zero or \"no heartbeat\" means one-shot; one host-native carrier, else same-session sleep, never both. Host beats are event-driven (Heartbeat). |\n117: | Permissions | Per platform, not one global bypass. Honor explicit permission-mode overrides. Ordinary approval leftovers are handled here within authorized scope, not routinely sent to the human. |\n118: | Cursor | Never use `/model` as a read-only probe. |\n119: \n120: Bind the consuming project's canonical project root once at setup with\n121: `export KAOLA_PROJECT_RUNNER_CANONICAL_REPO=<abs root>`, then omit `--repo`; the\n122: Runner fills it and refuses a drifted root at start. Ask each worker to invoke its installed\n123: workflow-next. Without that binding, linked-worktree starts and existing-run\n124: recovery are Agent decisions, not transport gates. See\n125: [workflow-worktree.md](references/workflow-worktree.md).\n126: \n127: Model mismatches are evidence, not automatic start gates. Bypass is not broader authorization. With no verified ACP skip-all,\n128: permission may still arise: `permit` settles it; never add a gate.\n129: \n130: ## Heartbeat\n131: \n132: The heartbeat is the working prompt itself: a Host (any platform with a\n133: `host_skill_entry`) runs it on each worker event; Codex's own timer serves only\n134: a non-Host Codex supervisor; host-native carriers. Its `body` is one JSON object per\n135: [heartbeat-skeleton.md](references/heartbeat-skeleton.md), written\n136: only by `state`. It is the effective-now snapshot, not a\n137: log: update changed facts/duties, replacing superseded quota, priority and\n138: plans, keeping in-flight locators and unfinished duties. A confirmed change\n139: applies in that beat; decision-boundary adoption:\n140: [duty-reconcile.md](references/duty-reconcile.md); a lowered quota alone cancels nothing. A report-only\n141: request disables execution actions. When an updated Skill loads, reconcile the\n142: heartbeat once with current rules, latest valid instructions and fresh seat\n143: facts, keeping user limits and the frontier (v1: `state migrate`).\n144: \n145: After close-out, cancel the native heartbeat or stop scheduling the next\n146: sleep. Having no ready task now is not\n147: project completion. Do not hard-code other hosts' scheduler APIs.\n148: \n149: ## Delivery\n150: \n151: Prefer the selected, authorized Workflow sync/merge when a PR is not required:\n152: a PR is not opened merely for handoff when that sink is suitable.\n153: If PRs exist, advance actionable ones first on contested suitable\n154: capacity; other authorized work continues in parallel across permitted CLIs.\n155: A blocked PR keeps an owner and next action without a global hold. Honor\n156: acceptance, explicit PR requests, branch protection, stop-intake, selected\n157: sink, and write ownership.\n158: \n159: ## Main execution loop\n160: \n161: 1. **Recover and observe.** Load compact current state as needed on a new\n162:    turn or recovery; reuse facts already read this turn. Read only fresh\n163:    worker/run, Git/Forge and Runner facts relevant to the next decision. Use exact owned sessions and\n164:    current platform Skills. Observe busy workers without injecting \"status?\"\n165:    messages or polling raw frames as a human UI. Do not replay a prompt whose\n166:    acceptance or effects are known or uncertain; investigate the existing action\n167:    first. Steering a running turn is an Agent\n168:    choice, never a Runner policy: pick the mode yourself, read the receipt, and\n169:    never call a not-consumed or `unknown` steer delivered.\n170: 2. **Decide and dispatch.** Resolve worker questions within existing\n171:    authorization; escalate only major structural, value, or extra-authority\n172:    decisions. `HUMAN_DECISION_REQUIRED` is considered by the orchestrator\n173:    first. A seat whose `status` says `stale: true` is not a dispatch target;\n174:    an operator-confirmed exception on that one `send`/`steer` is the\n175:    orchestrator's own call; replace it with `drain-restart` at idle\n176:    (zcode-host-dispatch.md).\n177:    Examine authorized remaining work. Give each clear task directly to a suitable authorized worker as a new session, chosen from the current eligible-candidate projection by owner direction, class responsibility, profile/task fit and capacity; past dispatch or success informs that choice, never replaces it. Split or parallelize when independent parts gain real time or coverage. The count is a ceiling, not a target to fill; never invent work or expand authorization. At the hard cap, stop one seat before starting any new one (stop-before-start).\n178:    Before planning, assigning or judging QA, read\n179:    [qa-evidence.md](references/qa-evidence.md) unless the current version is\n180:    already in context. Planned dispatch (research, QA, report or\n181:    implementation) uses `execute`\n182:    ([dispatch-collect.md](references/dispatch-collect.md)): it starts absent\n183:    seats at the preset `--tier` and records their facts. Direct Runner\n184:    `start`/`send` is standalone, degraded or same-assignment recovery\n185:    (zcode-host-dispatch.md). No permission-mode override unless the human\n186:    wrote one. One dispatch prompt per ready session. External knowledge gap behind a route, obstacle, failure or QA question: [public-research.md](references/public-research.md). You still accept and exact-stop. State the task,\n187:    working location, write ownership, constraints, delivery requirements, and\n188:    the doc-impact call in its prompt, plus whether Workflow is on. That prompt\n189:    carries the assignment only: never orchestration policy or the seat roster.\n190:    The worker must not self-finalize before acceptance; irreversible or value\n191:    choices print `HUMAN_DECISION_REQUIRED` and wait. Merely seeing a\n192:    worktree or ledger is not write authorization. Same-file collaboration needs explicit\n193:    coordination and an integrator, not a blanket disjointness rule. Do not\n194:    expand the authorized goal or duplicate claims. Pass still-valid candidate\n195:    evidence; recheck changed/invalidated facts or actual gaps. Keep required\n196:    project/release checks. Substantive failure: [task-failure.md](references/task-failure.md).\n197:    One run claims one real issue: never a bundle claim, worktree, ledger or\n198:    session spanning several, and collaborating workers share that issue's run under\n199:    distinct names. Choose the real open issue before start, name the session\n200:    `<platform>-<CODE>-i<ISSUE>-<purpose>` (`droid-KT-i274-parser`) with `CODE` the\n201:    heartbeat's declared project short code, verify it in the start receipt, and\n202:    keep the rule on later dispatches and restarts. Hosts, diagnostics and\n203:    issue-less tasks carry no issue number and never an invented one. Detail:\n204:    [issue-dispatch.md](references/issue-dispatch.md).\n205: 3. **Accept the delivery.** Mission-frontier done triggers review, not automatic finalize. When a worker\n206:    claims completion, judge its actual diff, checks, docs and run records\n207:    against that assignment under the effective global Workflow rules. Reuse\n208:    sufficient evidence for this candidate; return only concrete routine deviations,\n209:    omissions or invalidated evidence to that worker, and never finalize on\n210:    incomplete evidence or lowered assertions. Worker prose, idle, green CI or\n211:    a successful script exit is not acceptance, and acceptance is not project\n212:    QA: you pick when aggregate QA/doc checks run; unrun ones stay pending duties.\n213: 4. **Finalize and synchronize.** Acceptance authorizes that candidate's\n214:    pending finalize: direct its owning worker to finalize and merge, then verify\n215:    remote, Issue, archive, doc docking, and cleanup results: lifecycle facts,\n216:    not QA PASS ([doc-maintenance](references/doc-maintenance.md)).\n217:    Prefer one run at a time; judge safe concurrency. After the baseline moves, direct affected owners to save\n218:    work and rebase/update at a safe boundary. Review conflicts and revalidate changes; do not switch HEAD\n219:    mid-measurement or reuse invalidated evidence. History rewriting needs applicable authorization.\n220:    When records are inconsistent, investigate and direct a scoped repair;\n221:    never fabricate claim identities.\n222: 5. **Reclaim seats and report.** The only legal idle seat is one whose delivery is\n223:    awaiting acceptance; a rejected delivery's repair is the same assignment unless substantive (task-failure),\n224:    and an accepted seat keeps only the finalize/cleanup duties it owns. Once\n225:    it owns none (done or explicitly handed off) or the seat is abandoned,\n226:    exact-stop it in that same beat. The stop action is the exact owned session\n227:    `stop` via the matching platform Skill, which ends its ACP holder. Idle is not\n228:    keep-alive or completion. New authorized work or another task gets a fresh\n229:    `start` under a new standard name, never a finished seat;\n230:    `--resume`/`--continue` recover the same assignment only.\n231: \n232:    A Host with in-flight/delivery/acceptance/close-out is not an idle worker;\n233:    keep it; send it no \"continue\". Next beat: worker event. Before quiescence\n234:    reconcile owner/heartbeat project.goal/stop and QA/docs; worker done ends no\n235:    open mandate. [duty-reconcile.md](references/duty-reconcile.md).\n236:    Claim only with intake open. Blocked duties keep next action/reopening condition\n237:    in existing heartbeat values. Assignment-local retry bounds never pause project;\n238:    owner/project bounds keep scope. Worker stop/replacement never resets attempt limits.\n239:    Ending a run: stop/pause/cleanup. Session stop is not issue closure;\n240:    acceptance/merge/cleanup differ.\n241: \n242: ## Ending a run\n243: \n244: When ending a project run, finish every in-hand authorized task and each\n245: claimed/in-flight issue of this run; merge their worktrees/branches, remove\n246: branch tails, and clean the workspace per Kaola Workflow close-out. Do not park\n247: unfinished branches as the normal end.\n248: \n249: Do not accept or dispatch new tasks after the stop boundary. Do not claim or\n250: start new issues. After time-up, still finish in-hand and merge/clean.\n251: \n252: The time/condition boundary is not the scoped pause. Only an explicit owner\n253: \"stop here and continue later\" with stated scope skips cleanup and permits\n254: recovery-preserving branches; do not merge or clean beyond that scope.\n255: \n256: ## Report\n257: \n258: Use the user's report format; otherwise one compact current-work table plus\n259: outstanding close-out and pending QA items. Include task/progress, meaningful\n260: model mismatches, blockers, and next action. For non-pool seats, per platform report `live N /\n261: authorized M` and the seats stopped this beat; N > M with no stop that beat\n262: violates the cap. Pool seats report live seats marked exempt. Drop stale stopped rows from reports. Keep duties traceable in\n263: existing records and reuse existing Runner `stop`/`start`: no session state\n264: machine, quota engine, or close-out dashboard or ledger.", "type": "text"}, "type": "content"}], "locations": [{"path": "/Users/ylmacstudio/.config/opencode/skills/kaola-project-runner/SKILL.md"}], "rawOutput": {"metadata": {"truncated": false}}, "sessionUpdate": "tool_call_update", "status": "completed", "toolCallId": "call_00_3w1e8zbm4ts1uhw7q6n8twm1"}}
